      "instruction": "Follow docs/development-flow.md and docs/review-guidelines.md. Work only in your assigned worktree. Do not delegate or spawn subagents. Do not invoke live agent tests, push, publish, comment/review on GitHub or merge remote state; explicit GitWeave System Actions handle publication. Never redeem usage-limit reset tickets, buy allowance, or switch models/providers to bypass limits. Stop and return blocked if an actual permission/capability limitation prevents completion; do not invent an unsupported workaround or weaken requirements. Ordinary code/test defects are fixable findings, not capability blockers. Keep project material in English. Use deterministic tests and preserve existing behavior outside the issue's explicit change. Do not alter other worktrees or the original checkout.\n\nImplement Issue #17 completely: durable remote persistence and recovery of GitWeave-owned provenance. Reuse current Git refs/notes and runtime-owned credentials; no database, cloud service or agent-side publication authority. Define and document a small deterministic destination contract (explicit remote selection where needed, existing PR/publication repository and/or configured origin as appropriate). Preserve meaningful local/offline behavior when no remote exists, but a Run with a resolved provenance destination must not silently claim durable success after upload fails. Avoid silently sending provenance to a repository unrelated to its artifact Run.\n\nAt least completed Runs with a destination must publish the FINAL Run record, notes and all current-Run owned attempt/input refs, including failed attempts in a successful retry and the final merge/System Action result. An early publish_pr snapshot alone is insufficient. Use runtime finalization or another minimal mechanism that includes the final state without recursive self-recording. Keep artifact branches separate and unchanged by provenance publication. Operate only on the exact current Run's owned namespaces; never mirror/push unrelated refs, other Runs, tags or branches. Do not expose credential values in URLs, error output, config or new records. Logs already retained for the selected Run are part of its explicitly requested provenance; do not add a generic secret-scrubbing or permission framework.\n\nProvide a usable GitWeave persistence/recovery entry point (including exporting an already completed local Run, so a Run started with an older runtime can be durably archived afterward). A fresh local repository must be able to recover a selected historical Run and its notes/attempts after the original local repository is deleted. Include discoverable/documented fetch refspecs or CLI usage. Preserve ownership and avoid clobbering unrelated local or remote refs; detect remote collisions/partial publication and make repeated export/fetch safe. Use atomic publication where supported or a clearly diagnosed contract; do not hide rejected pushes or authentication failures. Preserve local evidence when persistence fails. No unbounded retries, branch-protection bypass, or changing repository settings.\n\nAdd deterministic integration tests using temporary local bare remotes and fresh clones: completed final Run export, successful and failed-attempt notes, PR input refs, final action result, remote persistence after artifact integration/branch deletion, recovery after source deletion, no unrelated refs pushed, idempotency/conflicts, missing destination and remote errors. Mock external GitHub operations. Update CLI tests/docs/examples as appropriate without pulling in unrelated open Issue #7. No actual remote test data or comments; workflow-owned publication is handled by System Actions. Keep the implementation focused and report ready only when the full Issue is covered and tests pass.\n\nImplement the whole supplied issue. Run all relevant deterministic checks. Leave final files for GitWeave to checkpoint. Return ready only after completion, or blocked with concrete details.",
